# Developer-facing language

## Principle

Anything a human reads as **git history, PR review, README, changelog, or agent status
summary** must make sense in short plain English without decoding internal codes.

Tracking IDs remain valid in **session docs, execution plans, workflow-state, ADRs, and
corpus citations**. When they appear in developer-facing prose, pair them with English
(or put them in parentheses after the English).

## Where this applies

| Surface | Do | Do not |
|---------|----|--------|
| Commit subject | `feat: enqueue catch-up for failed embeds (F75)` | `[T127.5] feat: …` / `T01: …` |
| PR title | `Corpus automations catch-up (F75)` | `[M127] F75 catch-up` as the only readable title |
| PR / changelog body | English bullets; cite `F75`, `ADR-052`, task ids | Bullets that are only codes |
| README / LOCAL_DEV / ops docs | Explain what and why in English | Lead sections with opaque codes alone |
| Agent “completed” reports | `Completed: add export endpoint. Next: …` | `Completed T3.2` with no English |
| Session / plan tables | IDs as keys + English intent columns | Rewriting historical IDs away |

## Keep as citations (with English)

- Features: `F75`, `F77`
- ADRs: `ADR-052`
- Sessions / decisions: `S030`, `S030-D29`, `RD-334`
- Tests / journeys: `TC-264`, `UJ-012`
- Bugs / issues: `BUG-2026-…`, `#192`
- Evolve cycles: `EV-027` (fine in branch names; PR title still needs English)

## Avoid as the primary message

- Task / milestone leads: `T1.1`, `[T{id}]`, `[M1]`, `EBO1`-style opaque prefixes
- Status lines that are only codes (`PRR-001`, `PRM-001`, `RET-001`) without a short English gloss
- Changelogs grouped solely by bare `[T…]` / `[M…]` prefixes

## Session and plan docs

Internal IDs in paths, tables, and cross-links are fine. Prefer:

> Catch-up enqueues residual embeds only (`job_type=automation_catchup`) — [Corpus: feature-list.md §F75], ADR-052.

Not:

> F75 / RD-334 / T127.5 done.
